WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.520
Searches, forms, bookmarks, and extensions reveal convenience features that can become important evidence sources.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:14.020
Search engine queries may show research, navigation attempts, troubleshooting, intent signals, or simple curiosity.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.840
Form history and autofill artifacts may show repeated entries or user convenience behavior.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:25.820
Bookmarks and favorites may show saved resources, work applications, or personal interests.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.520
Search artifacts require careful interpretation.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:35.860
A query can be typed, suggested, repeated, synchronized, or generated as part of a browser or search workflow.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.760
It may show that a phrase was entered, but it does not always show why.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:44.780
A search result page may load many resources, redirects, or previews.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.720
Analysts should distinguish the query itself from the pages that were subsequently visited.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.220
Form history and autofill can be highly privacy sensitive.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:02.640
They may contain names, addresses, email addresses, phone numbers, search terms, organization names, or other personal and business data.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:08.780
Investigators should limit review to authorized scope and avoid reporting unnecessary personal information.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.320
Autofill existence does not automatically prove that the same user typed a value during the investigation window.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:21.540
Extensions matter because they can affect browsing behavior, permissions, storage, and application interaction.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:37.040
Installed extension lists, extension permissions, extension storage, update records, and configuration data can help explain redirects, content modification, password-manager behavior, downloads, notifications, or unusual browser activity.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.780
An extension should not be labeled malicious by default; its behavior must be analyzed and corroborated.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.400
Browser notifications can also be useful.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:51.300
They may show that a web application had permission to send alerts or that a site produced a notification.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.400
But notification presence does not always prove the user saw, opened, or acted on it.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:03.740
Like other browser artifacts, these convenience features are most useful when they are tied to a timeline and supported by other evidence.
